<worksheet xml:id="ws_5_3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>5.3 Practice Worksheet</title>


<introduction>
    <p>
    <url href="https://youtu.be/XOX9l0s6_Qk">Click here for a walkthrough video of some problem below.</url>
    </p>
</introduction>


<exercisegroup>
<title>Exponential Functions</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Could the table represent an exponential function <m>y=A\cdot b^t</m>? Why or why not? For those that could, find the formula.
</p>
</introduction>


<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>y</m></cell>
<cell><m>81</m></cell>
<cell><m>27</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The inputs are evenly spaced, and we multiply each output by <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> to go to the next one in the table. Therefore, this function is exponential.

The initial value is <m>81</m>, so we have <m>y=A b^t</m>. Plugging in a point, say <m>(1,27)</m>, tells us that
<md>
27=81 \cdot b^1
</md> so <m>b=\frac{27}{81}=\frac{1}{3}</m>. Therefore
<md>
y=81\cdot (1 /3)^t.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>y</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The inputs are evenly spaced, but we do not have a constant number that multiplies the outputs in the table. Indeed, the outputs are also evenly spaced (by <m>2</m>). Therefore this function is actually <em>linear</em>, and not exponential.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>y</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>2.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>12.5</m></cell>
<cell><m>62.5</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The inputs are evenly spaced (by <m>3</m>). There is a constant multiplier between outputs of <m>5</m>. <em>To find this number, divide each pair of outputs: <m>\frac{2.5}{0.5} =5</m>, <m>\frac{12.5}{2.5} =5</m>, and <m>\frac{62.5}{12.5} =5</m></em>. Therefore this function is exponential.

The initial value is not provided, so we make two equations from two points and divide them. We have
<md>
2.5 = A \cdot b^2 \quad \text{and} \quad 0.5 = A \cdot b^{-1}.
</md> Dividing these yields
<md>
\frac{2.5}{0.5}  = \frac{A\cdot b^2}{A\cdot b^{-1}} \quad \implies \quad 5 = b^3 \quad \implies \quad b = \sqrt[3]{5}=1.7099 .
</md> Therefore <m>y=A \cdot (1.7099)^t</m> and we plug in a point, say <m>(-1,0.5)</m>, to solve for <m>A</m>:
<md>
0.5 = A \cdot (1.7099)^{-1} \quad \implies \quad A = \frac{0.5}{(1.7099)^{-1}}  = 0.8549.
</md> So the equation is
<md>
y = 0.8549 \cdot (1.7099)^t.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>-4</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>11</m></cell>
<cell><m>16</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
<cell><m>2.2</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.8</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.4</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.0</m></cell>
<cell><m>0.6</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The inputs are evenly spaced, however there is not a constant ratio between outputs. Rather, we subtract <m>0.4</m> from each output to go to the next. Therefore the function is actually <em>linear</em> and not exponential.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>g(t)</m></cell>
<cell><m>96</m></cell>
<cell><m>24</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The inputs are evenly spaced, and there is a constant ratio of <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> between the outputs. <em>To determine this, compute <m>\frac{24}{96} =\frac{1}{4}</m>, <m>\frac{6}{24}=\frac{1}{4}</m>, and <m>\frac{1.5}{6} =\frac{1}{4}</m>.</em> Therefore the function is exponential.

To find its formula, note that <m>A=96</m> was given. Then <m>g(t)=96 \cdot b^t</m> and we solve for <m>b</m> using another point, say <m>(3,24)</m>:
<md>
24 = 96\cdot b^3 \quad \implies \quad b^3 = 0.25 \quad \implies \quad b = \sqrt[3]{0.25} = 0.62996 .
</md> Therefore the formula is
<md>
g(t) = 96 \cdot (0.62996)^t.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
<cell><m>12</m></cell>
<cell><m>15</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>h(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-6</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The inputs are evenly spaced, however at a first glance this cannot be exponential because we notice that the function is increasing and then decreasing. Exponential functions cannot do this; they always increase or always decrease. So this is <em>not exponential</em>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>




</exercisegroup>

</worksheet>
